# Introducing Terraform locally with Minikube

This plan outlines how to integrate Terraform into this project using your local Minikube cluster (with no cloud provider). It is designed to teach you Infrastructure as Code (IaC) best practices and serve as a strong resume builder.

We will translate the existing Kubernetes YAML manifests (`k8/*`) into structured, DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) Terraform files using a custom reusable Terraform module.

---

## Architectural Approach (Local IaC)

Even without a cloud provider like AWS, GCP, or Azure, Terraform is widely used to manage local Kubernetes environments and third-party tools via **Kubernetes & Helm Providers**.

Here is how the control flow works:

```mermaid
graph TD
    A[Local Machine / VS Code] -->|terraform apply| B(Terraform CLI)
    B -->|Communicates via kubeconfig| C(Minikube K8s Control Plane)
    C -->|Orchestrates| D[Pods, Deployments, Services]
    C -->|Deploys CRDs| E[Strimzi Kafka Operators]
    C -->|Persistent Volumes| F[Local Disk Mounts]
```

### Key Highlights for Your Resume
* **Infrastructure as Code (IaC)**: Eliminating manual `kubectl apply` commands in favor of standard declarative state management.
* **Reusable Terraform Modules**: Creating a custom module to package microservices (ports, replicas, configs, secrets) rather than duplicating blocks.
* **Kubernetes Custom Resource Definitions (CRDs) via Terraform**: Deploying Strimzi Kafka operators and resources natively in HCL using `kubernetes_manifest`.
* **State Management**: Understanding Terraform state file tracking, drift detection, and local backends.

